      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 11 & -1 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 35 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above and below $a_{23}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 11 & -1 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{1}:=R_{1}-R_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 11 & -1 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{3}:=R_{3}+4R_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 35 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{1}:=R_{1}-R_{2}$ and $R_{3}:=R_{3}+4R_{2}$.",
